/**

@page toolbox_loadbalancing Load Balancing (Domain Decomposition)

\tableofcontents

The load balancing toolbox distributes Peano 4 spacetrees across ranks and
threads.

- You tell Peano to run through the mesh.
- Every now and then, you invoke the load balancing toolbox which might,
  hidden away from you, trigger splits.
- You continue to invoke mesh traversals, and these then realise the
  triggered splits.

## Usage through Python API

Many extensions, such as ExaHyPE 2, use the load balancing toolbox and offer a
tailored load balancing configuration,
i.e. they provide means for users to employ the toolbox without interfering
with the main functions or calling the load balancing manually.
Indeed, the toolbox follows the philosophy that the chosen load balancing
determines the load balancing strategy, while the configuration object
tailors this strategy to your experimental setup and machine.

- See more on how to @ref page_exahype_multicore "configure load balancing within ExaHyPE 2".
- Swift 2 has its own parallelisation documentation in repositories that ship Swift.

## The load balancing state machine

The toolbox ships a single load balancer, toolbox::loadbalancing::LoadBalancer,
which is a small state machine (toolbox::loadbalancing::State). Its behaviour is
implemented by two initial split strategies that run one after the other:

- **InitialInterRankSplit** spreads the domain across the MPI ranks: a populated
  rank forks cells off its heaviest local tree onto ranks that still hold no
  tree.
- **InitialIntraRankSplit** spreads the work across the threads/cores of a rank:
  it decomposes the heaviest local tree into several sibling trees so every core
  can be kept busy.

The balancer enters **Stagnation** when the initial strategies have no valid
action. On a completed uniform mesh, this follows a settled exact tree target.
On an adaptive mesh, hanging vertices can make remaining slots unreachable,
so stagnation can occur below the target. The remaining states
(InterRankBalancing, IntraRankBalancing) are placeholders; dynamic rebalancing
and tree joining are not implemented.

Both strategies finalise on the last mesh that is guaranteed to be uniform:
cells at adaptive refinement boundaries carry hanging vertices and can never
fork, so once the runner reports that every leaf has reached the configured
minimum level (toolbox::loadbalancing::LoadBalancer::notifyUniformGridComplete()),
the strategies use the completed uniform mesh without waiting for a larger
piece size. If the configured target exceeds the globally reduced leaf count,
the run terminates before another split and reports the requested and available
counts. The target is not silently capped. Imbalance that adaptive refinement
introduces after the initial decomposition remains because the required tree
joins are not implemented.

# Implementation remarks

The load balancer is composed of a few collaborating classes:

- A blacklist (toolbox::loadbalancing::Blacklist) which cools a tree down for a
  few sweeps after it has been split,
- A configuration (toolbox::loadbalancing::Configuration) which tailors the
  strategy to the setup and machine (maximum number of trees per rank and split
  mode),
- The state (toolbox::loadbalancing::State),
- Statistics (toolbox::loadbalancing::Statistics) which gather per-tree values
  and explicitly reduce the global counts. The balancer completes the preceding
  reduction before each decision and starts the next one afterwards.

The cost metric is currently fixed: a tree's load is its number of local
unrefined cells. Please consult the individual class documentations for further
details.

## Tailoring the load balancing

You do not swap out whole strategies. Instead you tailor the built-in
distribution phases through a toolbox::loadbalancing::Configuration object.
ExaHyPE 2 for example provides exahype2::LoadBalancingConfiguration, which feeds
the user's runtime parameters (maximum number of trees per rank and periodic
boundary handling) into the strategies.

@image html domain-decomposition/tree-topology.png

The timing, target, and mode of each split all matter. Peano supports two
split modes: bottom-up along the space-filling curve, and top-down within
the spacetree. The latter approach is similar to recursive kd-decomposition.
Each approach comes along with pros and cons. SFC-based partitioning makes
it easier to have well-balanced domains, but the arising subdomain topology
(who is adjacent to whom and what happens with coarser levels) can become
tricky. Furthermore, you will have to know your mesh structure completely
to construct good SFC subpartitions.
Recursive top-down decomposition lends itself to domain decomposition which
kicks in directly while we create the tree.

The load balancer's configuration (toolbox::loadbalancing::Configuration)
selects the splitting scheme for the current state. Before you dig
into this, we strongly recommend to
read through @ref page_peano_domain_decomposition "Peano's domain decomposition remarks",
and maybe even to read the paper

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
@article{Weinzierl:2019:Peano,
  author =       {T. Weinzierl},
  title =        {The Peano software---parallel, automaton-based, dynamically adaptive grid traversals},
  journal =      {ACM Transactions on Mathematical Software},
  year =         {2019},
  volume =       {45},
  number =       {2},
  pages =        {14}
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

which is <a href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3319797">available through the gold access route</a>.

## Rebalancing, joins and AMR

The initial strategies create trees through splits and never issue joins. The
toolbox does not implement dynamic rebalancing with joins. The Peano core can
join a degenerate child tree, which hosts exclusively unrefined cells, back
into its parent to permit grid coarsening.

This strict commitment to tree-like joins and forks makes the
multiscale implementation manageable. You can still
oversubscribe the threads, i.e. create more logical subpartitions than
threads, and hence mimic diffusion-based load balancing for example.

Dynamic AMR can propagate through a domain cut. However, Peano never
deploys a cell to another tree if that cell hosts a hanging vertex
and its parent cell is not deployed to the same tree, too. This constrains
notably the SFC-based bottom-up splitting. You might encounter situations
where the AMR effectively "stops" the SFC decomposition from adding further
cells to a new domain. The reason for this policy is that the tracking of
multiscale data consistency between hanging nodes on different trees
otherwise becomes unmanageably hard.

## Performance flaws

Inappropriate or problematic domain decomposition decisions manifest in
performance flaws. There are a few further resources that
discuss generic details which are not tied to this toolbox. However, they
are very useful to get the most out of this toolbox:

- Peano has a @ref page_peano_runtime_analysis "dedicated runtime analysis discussion" which
  can help to understand the runtime behaviour shaped by the domain
  decomposition.
- Peano has a dedicated page on @ref page_peano_performance_optimisation "performance optimisation".
  It discusses many flaws and how to fix them. The sections below discuss the
  rationale and provide background information on why things work the way
  they do.

Further to the generic Peano discussions, individual extensions have their
own specific discussion of how to analyse and to understand performance
characteristics, which in turn again might relate to domain decomposition
decisions:

- Peano's @ref page_exahype_runtime_analysis "runtime analysis discussion" and its @ref page_exahype_performance_optimisation "performance optimisation recipes".
- Swift's own performance optimisation discussion in repositories that ship Swift.

# Tuning and tailoring the domain decomposition

While the toolbox offers generic load balancing, it cannot accommodate all
applications because it lacks
domain-specific knowledge. However, there are generic tuning knobs and
considerations. The page below summarises some generic rationale and
lessons learned. Particular flaws and their fixes are discussed on the
@ref page_peano_performance_optimisation "Peano optimisation page".
So we get an explanation of things here, why things work the way they
do. The recipes how to spot flaws and how to fix them are discussed on the
generic page.

## Load balancing throughout mesh construction

Every load balancing strategy in Peano 4 balances two competing objectives:

- Load balancing has to happen as soon as possible throughout the grid
  construction process, as we want the grid construction to run in parallel.
  Splits themselves are also memory-demanding---at least temporarily
  as parts of a partition have to be replicated and moved around---so we
  prefer setups that perform these splits before we have a lot of mesh
  data in place that has to be moved around.
- Load balancing performs best if triggered for reasonably big meshes,
  as finer meshes allow for better tree cuts. If we know how many cells
  there are, we can balance these cells better, and we can subdivide cells
  more accurately: If we deploy a 3x3 grid to two cores, one core will get
  5 and one 4 cells. If all cells refine once more, this will induce a
  45 vs 36 mesh. A balanced cut of a 9x9 mesh gives a 41 vs
  40 mesh. So the more we know, i.e. the closer the constructed mesh to
  the intended final mesh, the better the domain.

The first argument is a strict one. If we run out of memory, the
simulation crashes. The second argument is a desirable one.

The shipped initial splits resolve the trade-off as follows (see
@ref page_toolbox_loadbalancing_strategies "the strategies page" for the
details):

- Spread over the ranks as early as the mesh supports it, so the grid
  construction itself runs in parallel and forks move little data.
- Before uniform-grid completion, delay each cut to the first level where every
  target piece holds at least one coarse-cell-worth of fine cells, so the
  floor/ceil imbalance of the cut stays bounded.
- Hold the grid static while forks deploy: refining underneath an in-flight
  fork makes the fork grab cells of the wrong resolution, and rebalancing is
  memory-hungry enough without extra refinement.
- Provision one tree per thread by default (`-trees`); oversubscribing a rank
  with more trees than threads is the user's choice.

## Split modes: bottom-up vs. top-down

A split is parameterised by a mode (peano4::grid::SplitInstruction::Mode),
chosen by toolbox::loadbalancing::Configuration::getMode():

- **BottomUp** picks cells along the space-filling curve from the fine grid
  upwards. It produces accurate, SFC-compact cuts and is the mode used by all
  shipped configurations. A cell carrying a hanging vertex whose
  parent lives on another tree must not be deployed (see the constraint
  discussion above). A rejected requested split terminates the run; partial
  splits are not accepted. The initial strategies stop issuing requests when
  adaptive hanging vertices make the remaining slots unreachable.
- **AggressiveTopDown** descends the tree and moves whole refined subtrees at
  once, so it can cross a refinement boundary at subtree granularity rather than
  stalling on individual fine cells. It is, however, **experimental**: it is not
  selected by any shipped configuration and has no test coverage, so treat it as
  work in progress.

For the regular and mildly graded meshes in the shipped configurations,
BottomUp is the default. Robust handling of strongly graded AMR through a
hardened top-down mode, tree joining, or dynamic rebalancing is not implemented.

# Strategies

There is a dedicated subpage
\subpage page_toolbox_loadbalancing_strategies which describes the splitting
problem and the shipped strategies in detail.

